{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788228442036,"sessionID":"ses_fa54e4cfeffeF35792XylHPXI3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_00720a3b752b40feaed216c3","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"./venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport csv\nwith open('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv', newline='', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nclubs = {}\nfor r in rows:\n    c = r['Club'].strip()\n    if c and any(t in r['Nationality'] for t in ('Brazil',)):\n        clubs.setdefault(c, 0)\n        clubs[c] += 1\n# brazilian players per club, look for flamengo-like\nfor c, n in sorted(clubs.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1])[:25]:\n    print(repr(c), n)\nprint('---all clubs containing flam/palme/santos/corinth:')\nfor c in sorted(clubs):\n    if any(t in c.lower() for t in ('flam','palme','santos','corinth','gremio','internacion')):\n        print(repr(c), clubs[c])\n\" 2>&1"},"output":"'Grêmio' 20\n'Atlético Mineiro' 20\n'Cruzeiro' 20\n'Fluminense' 20\n'Santos' 20\n'Internacional' 20\n'América FC (Minas Gerais)' 20\n'Botafogo' 20\n'Bahia' 20\n'Paraná' 20\n'Atlético Paranaense' 20\n'Vitória' 20\n'Sport Club do Recife' 20\n'Chapecoense' 20\n'Ceará Sporting Club' 20\n'Portimonense SC' 18\n'SC Braga' 13\n'CD Aves' 12\n'Rio Ave FC' 12\n'Clube Sport Marítimo' 10\n'GD Chaves' 10\n'Shakhtar Donetsk' 9\n'FC Porto' 8\n'Boavista FC' 8\n'Santa Clara' 8\n---all clubs containing flam/palme/santos/corinth:\n'Internacional' 20\n'Santos' 20\n'Santos Laguna' 1\n","metadata":{"output":"'Grêmio' 20\n'Atlético Mineiro' 20\n'Cruzeiro' 20\n'Fluminense' 20\n'Santos' 20\n'Internacional' 20\n'América FC (Minas Gerais)' 20\n'Botafogo' 20\n'Bahia' 20\n'Paraná' 20\n'Atlético Paranaense' 20\n'Vitória' 20\n'Sport Club do Recife' 20\n'Chapecoense' 20\n'Ceará Sporting Club' 20\n'Portimonense SC' 18\n'SC Braga' 13\n'CD Aves' 12\n'Rio Ave FC' 12\n'Clube Sport Marítimo' 10\n'GD Chaves' 10\n'Shakhtar Donetsk' 9\n'FC Porto' 8\n'Boavista FC' 8\n'Santa Clara' 8\n---all clubs containing flam/palme/santos/corinth:\n'Internacional' 20\n'Santos' 20\n'Santos Laguna' 1\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"./venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport csv\nwith open('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv', newline='', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nclubs = {}\nfor r in rows:\n    c = r['Club'].strip()\n    if c and any(t in r['Nationality'] for t in ('Brazil',)):\n        clubs.setdefault(c, 0)\n        clubs[c] += 1\n# brazilian players per club, look for flamengo-like\nfor c, n in sorted(clubs.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1])[:25]:\n    print(repr(c), n)\nprint('---all clubs containing flam/palme/santos/corinth:')\nfor c in sorted(clubs):\n    if any(t in c.lower() for t in ('flam','palme','santos','corinth','gremio','internacion')):\n        print(repr(c), clubs[c])\n\" 2>&1","time":{"start":1788228442019,"end":1788228442034}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The two sources record the same fixture on slightly different dates (e.g. Flamengo x Cruzeiro: 2019-04-28 vs 2019-04-27). These are the same matches (each team plays each other twice — 38 fixtures per team), so the date differs by a day. The dedup key requires exact date, so 7 leftovers remain.\n\nA better dedup approach: within the same competition+season, dedupe by (season, competition, unordered pair) — each pair meets at most twice per season in a league (home and away), so unordered pair isn't enough; need ordered pair (home, away) + season + competition. In a double round-robin, each ordered pair (A home, B away) appears exactly once. So dedup key = (competition, season, home_team, away_team) is sufficient for league matches — that's how I'd disambiguate. But for cup matches (two-legged ties, same pair same season, both orders...) or Libertadores group stage — ordered pair + season could collide in rare cases (e.g., same ordered pairing twice in a season in Copa do Brasil across rounds). Hmm.\n\nAlternative: prefer a source priority — keep one record per (competition, season, home, away, round-ish proximity)? Simpler robust approach: dedup by (competition, season, home, away, date ± window). E.g., group by (comp, season, ordered pair) and if multiple matches exist with dates within N days (say 7), treat as duplicates and keep the first by source priority. That handles one-day discrepancies while preserving legitimate repeated fixtures (different rounds usually weeks apart; two-legged cup ties within a week would be consecutive rounds which do happen — e.g. semifinal first leg and second leg within ~1 week, but those are same pair with swapped home/away, so ordered pair differs).\n\nCould an ordered (home,away) pair legitimately repeat within 7 days in the same competition+season? Two-legged ties: leg 1 is A home, leg 2 is B home — different ordered pairs. Libertadores group stage: A vs B twice, far apart. League: once each order. Replayed matches (abandoned)? Rare. I think a 7-day window on the same ordered pair + comp + season is safe.\n\nEven better: use a smaller window of 3 days. The observed discrepancy is 1 day. Let me use ±3 days to be safe.\n\nAlso should the union of both sources matter for correctness? If BR-Football has all 38 for 2019, then novo's 7 are pure duplicates. For older seasons (2003-2011), only novo exists, fine. For 2020-2022, only Brasileirao_Matches + BR-Football. So the best data comes from merging with dedup.\n\nWhat about goals differing between sources for the same match? Dedup by source priority: keep the first loaded per priority order. Priority should favor richer data (BR-Football has corners/shots — but we don't map those; actually we discard stats). Let me set source priority for keeping: prefer \"Brasileirao_Matches.csv\" (has round numbers) or BR-Football (has more metadata but we don't use it)? The current loader sets round=None for BR-Football rows. Hmm — keeping Brasileirao_Matches would preserve round info for 2012-2022. Let me use priority: novo_campeonato (2003-2019 has arena) > Brasileirao_Matches (has rounds) > BR-Football > others. But the same ordered pair within a season comes from exactly one of these per era mostly.\n\nActually, simpler and safer: keep the match whose date parses and prefer the source with more info. I'll implement priority order: (1) novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv, (2) Brasileirao_Matches.csv, (3) Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv, (4) Libertadores_Matches.csv, (5) BR-Football-Dataset.csv. Within a dedup group (comp, season, ordered pair, date-window), the highest-priority source wins.\n\nWait, but the loading order in _SOURCES currently is: novo, Brasileirao, Cup, Libertadores, BR-Football. I can implement windowed dedup keyed on ordered pair and pick priority. Let me write:\n\n```python\ndef _deduplicate(self) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Drop matches repeated across overlapping sources.\n\n    Two sources can record the same fixture one day apart, so a key of\n    (competition, season, home, away) within a +-3 day window is used;\n    the record from the highest-priority source is kept.\n    \"\"\"\n    SOURCE_PRIORITY = {name: i for i, name in enumerate(self._SOURCES)}\n    kept: list[Match] = []\n    seen: dict[tuple, tuple[date, int, int]] = {}  # key -> (date, priority, index in kept)\n    for match in self.matches:\n        priority = SOURCE_PRIORITY.get(match.source, 99)\n        if match.date and match.season:\n            key = (match.competition, match.season, identity_key(match.home_team), identity_key(match.away_team))\n            existing = seen.get(key)\n            if existing:\n                prev_date, prev_priority, prev_idx = existing\n                gap = abs((parse_date(match.date) - parse_date(prev_date)).days) -- hmm prev_date is a date\n```\n\nHmm, I store parsed dates. Let me restructure: store the date object.\n\n```python\n        if match.date and match.season:\n            key = (match.competition, match.season, identity_key(home), identity_key(away))\n            d = parse_date(match.date)  # match.date is already ISO string; parse again fine\n            existing = seen.get(key)\n            if existing is not None:\n                prev_date, prev_priority, prev_idx = existing\n                if d and prev_date and abs((d - prev_date).days) <= 3:\n                    if priority < prev_priority:\n                        kept[prev_idx] = match\n                        seen[key] = (d, priority, prev_idx)\n                    continue\n            seen[key] = (d, priority, len(kept))\n        kept.append(match)\n```\n\nWait, there's an ordering subtlety: `kept.append(match)` after setting seen. When replacing, we set kept[prev_idx] = match and continue (skip append). That works.\n\nBut there's a flaw: for cup competitions with two-legged ties — same ordered pair can't repeat, as discussed. Libertadores group stage: A home vs B once. OK. But wait — what about matches with the same ordered pair in the SAME season but genuinely different (e.g., a team that appears twice in group stage)? Libertadores group stage: each pair plays twice, once each venue. Fine.\n\nHowever, there's another subtle issue: same ordered pair, same season, but dates far apart — we keep both and then set seen[key] to the new one. But what if match 3 duplicates match 1 (not match 2)? E.g. A-B on Jan 1 (novo), A-B Jan 2 (BR-Football), A-B Jun 1 (legit second meeting? no — same ordered pair can't legitimately repeat in league, but in Copa do Brasil A-B could meet in round of 16 (March, A home) and... no, second leg is B home). For Libertadores: group stage has A home B (matchday 3) and... the reverse. OK, same ordered pair twice in one season+competition is essentially always a duplicate pair. But three meetings with the middle one far away: novo Jan 1, BR Jan 2, other-source Jun 1 — the Jun 1 one could be a legit different match in a knockout replay scenario... extremely unlikely. The window check compares against the LAST seen date for that key. If match 3 (Jun 1) is > 3 days from match 2's date, we keep it and update seen[key] to Jun 1. If a 4th match appears on Jun 2, it dedupes against Jun 1. Reasonable behavior.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a risk: Copa do Brasil same season, same ordered pair at e.g. \"third phase\" and \"quarterfinals\" if a bye/replay? Let me just check after implementing: count how many matches get dropped, and verify season 2019 Flamengo = 38.\n\n2. **Derby search bug**: in `search_derbies`, the pair comparison mixes display names with team_keys. Fix: compute identity_key for both sides:\n\n```python\npair = frozenset((identity_key(match.home_team), identity_key(match.away_team)))\n```\nand derby_keys built from identity_key too (or team_key of canonical names — canonical names' team_key == their text_key; using identity_key consistently is best).\n\nAlso `derby_name(a, b)` normalizes and looks up exact canonical pair — for head_to_head display. That's fine as long as dataset canonical names match DERBIES keys. \"Internaciona\" truncated display?? Interesting — display name shows 'Internaciona' (truncated in my print to 12 chars, not a real issue).\n\nBut wait — does `derby_name` work? It normalizes team_a/team_b then looks up pair. DERBIES keys are like (\"Flamengo\", \"Fluminense\") — normalize_team(\"Flamengo\") = \"Flamengo\" ✓. And a<b sorting: \"Botafogo\" < \"Fluminense\" ✓ etc. Let me verify pairs sort correctly: (\"Flamengo\",\"Fluminense\"): \"Flamengo\" < \"Fluminense\" ✓. (\"Corinthians\",\"Palmeiras\"): \"Corinthians\" < \"Palmeiras\" ✓. (\"Palmeiras\",\"São Paulo\"): \"Palmeiras\" < \"São Paulo\" ✓ (P < S). (\"Grêmio\",\"Internacional\"): normalize_team(\"Grêmio\")=\"Grêmio\", and \"Grêmio\" vs \"Internacional\": G < I ✓. (\"Bahia\",\"Vitória\"): B < V ✓. (\"Athletico Paranaense\",\"Coritiba\") ✓. (\"Ceará\",\"Fortaleza\") ✓. (\"Flamengo\",\"Vasco da Gama\") ✓. (\"Botafogo\",\"Vasco da Gama\") ✓. (\"Flamengo\",\"Botafogo\"): \"Botafogo\" < \"Flamengo\" — pair would be (\"Botafogo\",\"Flamengo\") but DERBIES has (\"Flamengo\",\"Botafogo\") ✗! And (\"Botafogo\",\"Fluminense\"): \"Botafogo\" < \"Fluminense\" ✓. (\"Botafogo\",\"Vasco da Gama\") ✓. So the (\"Flamengo\",\"Botafogo\") entry is wrongly ordered and never matches. Fix by sorting keys when defining or looking up. I'll fix derby_name to sort, and normalize DERBIES keys.\n\n3. **FIFA club naming**: check what clubs exist in fifa_data.csv for Brazilian clubs. Let me query.\n\n4. **Copa do Brasil finals**: check the round labels in the cup file, and season coverage (probably 2012-2021, so season=2023 has none — that's fine/expected).\n\nLet me look at the FIFA clubs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ab896f5001xRUj9SuzG4yE63","sessionID":"ses_fa54e4cfeffeF35792XylHPXI3","messageID":"msg_05ab7308b00118fcBGNmKzRsL5"}}
