{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788228709570,"sessionID":"ses_fa54e4cfeffeF35792XylHPXI3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_9a4e3f8e47e84fb6a6679e54","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"./venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter\nwith open('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv', newline='', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nclubs = Counter(r['Club'].strip() for r in rows if r['Club'].strip())\nbrazilish = [c for c in clubs if any(w in c.lower() for w in (\n 'américa','atletico','atlético','ceara','cruzeiro','chapecoense','bahia',\n 'botafogo','fluminense','gremio','grêmio','internacional','paraná','parana',\n 'recife','santos','vasco','vitoria','vitória','fortaleza','goias','goiás',\n 'sport','clube','futebol','esporte'))]\nfor c in sorted(brazilish): print(repr(c), clubs[c])\n\""},"output":"'América FC (Minas Gerais)' 20\n'América de Cali' 28\n'Atlético Bucaramanga' 28\n'Atlético Huila' 28\n'Atlético Madrid' 33\n'Atlético Mineiro' 20\n'Atlético Nacional' 28\n'Atlético Paranaense' 20\n'Atlético Tucumán' 28\n'Bahia' 20\n'Botafogo' 20\n'Ceará Sporting Club' 20\n'Chapecoense' 20\n'Club América' 30\n'Club Atlético Aldosivi' 28\n'Club Atlético Banfield' 27\n'Club Atlético Colón' 28\n'Club Atlético Huracán' 28\n'Club Atlético Lanús' 28\n'Club Atlético Talleres' 28\n'Club Atlético Tigre' 28\n'Clube Sport Marítimo' 23\n'Cruzeiro' 20\n'Fluminense' 20\n'Grêmio' 20\n'Internacional' 20\n'Paraná' 20\n'Real Sporting de Gijón' 30\n'Santos' 20\n'Santos Laguna' 26\n'Sport Club do Recife' 20\n'Sporting CP' 30\n'Sporting Kansas City' 27\n'Sporting Lokeren' 28\n'Sporting de Charleroi' 28\n'VfL Sportfreunde Lotte' 28\n'Vitória' 20\n'Vitória Guimarães' 30\n'Vitória de Setúbal' 28\n","metadata":{"output":"'América FC (Minas Gerais)' 20\n'América de Cali' 28\n'Atlético Bucaramanga' 28\n'Atlético Huila' 28\n'Atlético Madrid' 33\n'Atlético Mineiro' 20\n'Atlético Nacional' 28\n'Atlético Paranaense' 20\n'Atlético Tucumán' 28\n'Bahia' 20\n'Botafogo' 20\n'Ceará Sporting Club' 20\n'Chapecoense' 20\n'Club América' 30\n'Club Atlético Aldosivi' 28\n'Club Atlético Banfield' 27\n'Club Atlético Colón' 28\n'Club Atlético Huracán' 28\n'Club Atlético Lanús' 28\n'Club Atlético Talleres' 28\n'Club Atlético Tigre' 28\n'Clube Sport Marítimo' 23\n'Cruzeiro' 20\n'Fluminense' 20\n'Grêmio' 20\n'Internacional' 20\n'Paraná' 20\n'Real Sporting de Gijón' 30\n'Santos' 20\n'Santos Laguna' 26\n'Sport Club do Recife' 20\n'Sporting CP' 30\n'Sporting Kansas City' 27\n'Sporting Lokeren' 28\n'Sporting de Charleroi' 28\n'VfL Sportfreunde Lotte' 28\n'Vitória' 20\n'Vitória Guimarães' 30\n'Vitória de Setúbal' 28\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"./venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport csv\nfrom collections import Counter\nwith open('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv', newline='', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nclubs = Counter(r['Club'].strip() for r in rows if r['Club'].strip())\nbrazilish = [c for c in clubs if any(w in c.lower() for w in (\n 'américa','atletico','atlético','ceara','cruzeiro','chapecoense','bahia',\n 'botafogo','fluminense','gremio','grêmio','internacional','paraná','parana',\n 'recife','santos','vasco','vitoria','vitória','fortaleza','goias','goiás',\n 'sport','clube','futebol','esporte'))]\nfor c in sorted(brazilish): print(repr(c), clubs[c])\n\"","time":{"start":1788228709552,"end":1788228709569}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now the club-alias matching for players (helps `get_club_overview` and `search_players` club filter for FIFA club names like \"Sport Club do Recife\" vs match-data \"Sport Recife\"). Add a helper in normalize.py or queries.py. Let me add to queries.py a `club_match_key` function that maps a club name through _TEAM_ALIASES:\n\nActually _TEAM_ALIASES is private in normalize.py. I'll add a public function in normalize.py: `club_key(name)` that returns the canonical target if the text_key is in _TEAM_ALIASES, else None. Then in queries use it: try alias-canonical's text_key first, then plain text_key.\n\nLet me implement in queries.py:\n\n```python\ndef _club_keys(club: str) -> list[str]:\n    keys = []\n    canonical = resolve_club_alias(club)  # from normalize\n    if canonical:\n        keys.append(text_key(canonical))\n    keys.append(text_key(club))\n    return keys\n```\n\nHmm, simpler: add to normalize.py:\n\n```python\ndef club_alias(name: str) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"Canonical club name if the FIFA-style club spelling has an alias.\"\"\"\n    return _TEAM_ALIASES.get(text_key(name))\n```\n\nThen in queries.py `_match_club`: compute for each player the keys [text_key(club)] plus alias-resolved text_key if any. And wanted = set of keys for the requested club similarly.\n\nActually let me keep it simpler and robust:\n\n```python\n@staticmethod\ndef _club_keys(name: str) -> set[str]:\n    keys = {text_key(name)}\n    alias = club_alias(name)\n    if alias:\n        keys.add(text_key(alias))\n    return {k for k in keys if k}\n```\n\nAnd match if player keys ∩ wanted keys != empty, keeping the exact/prefix/contains preference: compute preference per player based on plain text_key only. Honestly, exact/prefix/contains ranking is nice but the alias case just needs to count as exact. Let me implement:\n\n```python\n@staticmethod\ndef _match_club(players, club):\n    wanted = QueryEngine._club_keys(club)\n    exact, prefix, contains = [], [], []\n    for player in players:\n        player_key = text_key(player.club)\n        if not player_key: continue\n        player_keys = QueryEngine._club_keys(player.club)\n        if player_keys & wanted:\n            exact.append(player)\n        elif player_key.startswith(next(iter(wanted))):  # hmm\n```\n\nThe startswith/contains on multi-key sets gets messy. Alternative: keep existing logic, but first translate the requested club to its canonical name via alias, then compare player club text_keys against canonical key as \"exact\":\n\n```python\n@staticmethod\ndef _match_club(players, club):\n    wanted_key = text_key(club)\n    alias = club_alias(club)\n    alias_key = text_key(alias) if alias else None\n    exact, prefix, contains = [], [], []\n    for player in players:\n        player_key = text_key(player.club)\n        if not player_key:\n            continue\n        alias_hit = alias_key and (player_key == alias_key or club_alias(player.club) == alias)\n        if player_key == wanted_key or alias_hit:\n            exact.append(player)\n        elif player_key.startswith(wanted_key):\n            prefix.append(player)\n        elif wanted_key in player_key:\n            contains.append(player)\n    return exact + prefix + contains\n```\n\nHmm, `club_alias(player.club)` calls per player — 18k players, fine (fast text ops).\n\nAnd in get_club_overview, the fuzzy matching blocks: replace text_key(club) comparisons with the alias-aware approach. Simplest: rewrite overview club matching using `_match_club`:\n\n```python\nplayers = QueryEngine._match_club(self.dataset.players, canonical)\n# but _match_club reorders exact+prefix+contains; and we want matched club labels\n```\n\nThat unifies behavior. matched_clubs = sorted({p.club for p in players}). Let me rewrite get_club_overview's player section:\n\n```python\nplayers = self._match_club(self.dataset.players, canonical)\nmatched_clubs = sorted({p.club for p in players})\nplayers.sort(key=lambda p: -p.overall)\n```\n\nWait but _match_club returns exact+prefix+contains concatenated — for overview that's fine.\n\nHmm, one concern: _match_club('Santos') — wanted_key 'santos'; player clubs 'Santos' exact ✓; 'Santos Laguna' startswith('santos') → prefix (also included). Slightly noisy but acceptable; exact first, sorted by overall anyway.\n\nAnother concern: get_club_overview('Barcelona')? Not relevant.\n\nNow what about \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" — club_alias: text_key = \"america fc minas gerais\" → in _TEAM_ALIASES → \"América Mineiro\". And match-data canonical \"América Mineiro\" — club_alias(\"América Mineiro\") → text_key \"america mineiro\" → alias → \"América Mineiro\" ✓ both resolve to same alias string. So get_club_overview(\"América Mineiro\") would find the FIFA squad ✓.\n\n\"Ceará Sporting Club\" → \"ceara sporting club\" → alias \"Ceará\" ✓; overview(\"Ceará\") → alias_key \"ceara\" — player_key \"ceara sporting club\" == alias_key? No. club_alias(\"Ceará Sporting Club\") = \"Ceará\" == alias \"Ceará\" ✓ exact hit ✓.\n\n\"Sport Club do Recife\" → \"sport club do recife\" → alias \"Sport Recife\" ✓; overview(\"Sport Recife\") → alias(\"Sport Recife\") → \"sport recife\" in aliases → \"Sport Recife\"; player club_alias(\"Sport Club do Recife\") = \"Sport Recife\" ✓.\n\n\"Atlético Paranaense\" → \"atletico paranaense\" → alias \"Athletico Paranaense\" ✓. \"Atlético Mineiro\" → ✓. \"Chapecoense\" ✓ direct. \"Cruzeiro\", \"Fluminense\", \"Santos\", \"Internacional\", \"Grêmio\", \"Botafogo\", \"Bahia\", \"Vitória\", \"Paraná\" ✓ direct.\n\nWhat about \"Atlético Goianiense\" (FIFA has it? maybe \"Atlético Goianiense\" 20 players)? alias \"atletico goianiense\" ✓ exists. \"Fortaleza\"? FIFA may have \"Fortaleza EC\" → alias \"fortaleza esporte clube\" exists but \"fortaleza ec\"? text_key \"fortaleza ec\" not in aliases; identity_key would handle but club matching uses text_key. Add \"fortaleza ec\": \"Fortaleza\" to aliases? Let me check FIFA club list more comprehensively and add needed aliases.\n\nAlso São Paulo FC in FIFA? Earlier search showed no \"sao paulo\" clubs at all. OK.\n\nLet me dump all FIFA clubs with >= 10 players to spot Brazilian ones and craft aliases.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05abcac0c001lyog0z1LmWU7V1","sessionID":"ses_fa54e4cfeffeF35792XylHPXI3","messageID":"msg_05abbbd36001m2v49SNbhYbOC9"}}
