{"type":"text","timestamp":1788229741298,"sessionID":"ses_fa54e4cfeffeF35792XylHPXI3","part":{"id":"prt_05acc3cdf001enOzPfjpoCAf6I","messageID":"msg_05acbf225001Jma4321AQLhkOV","sessionID":"ses_fa54e4cfeffeF35792XylHPXI3","type":"text","text":"All requirements are met. Summary of the repair:\n\n**Bugs fixed in the existing code** (root causes of the failed build):\n1. `data_loader.py` — `_load_matches` referenced an undefined `self._SOURCES`, so the dataset could never load; added the class-level `_SOURCES` tuple and removed a duplicated index assignment.\n2. `data_loader.py` — dedup required exact same-date matches, but overlapping sources record the same fixture a day apart (1,114 phantom duplicates; Flamengo \"109 pts / 45 games\" in 2019). Replaced with ordered-pair + competition + season ±3-day window dedup → 17,049 unique matches, and the 2019 Brasileirão table now matches reality exactly (Flamengo 90 pts, 38 games, complete).\n3. `queries.py` — `search_derbies` compared display names against team-key strings (always 0 results); now compares identity keys on both sides, plus fixed the unsorted `(\"Flamengo\",\"Botafogo\")` derby entry.\n4. Stage filter — \"final\" substring-matched \"semifinals\"; exact matches now take precedence.\n5. `normalize.py`/`queries.py` — FIFA formal club spellings (\"Sport Club do Recife\", \"Ceará Sporting Club\", …) never matched match-data names; added `club_alias()` used by player-club matching and `get_club_overview`.\n\n**Added:**\n- `tests/` — 125 tests (8 files) covering every requirement: normalization, all 6 CSV loads + dedup invariants, match/team/player/competition/stats/h2h queries (with independent recomputation cross-checks), BDD scenarios from the spec, MCP tool registration + `call_tool` tests, and an end-to-end stdio client round-trip against `python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp`. **95% coverage**.\n- `mcp` SDK was missing from the venv — installed; README documents setup, tools, and usage.\n\n**Verification:** `ruff check` clean, `pytest`: **125 passed**, standings sanity-checked against historical champions (2003 Cruzeiro, 2005 Corinthians, 2008/2019/2020 Flamengo), and the Libertadores-2019 final returns the real result (Flamengo 2–1 River Plate).","time":{"start":1788229729503,"end":1788229741295}}}
